<Layout id="performancelimitations" title="performancelimitations">
  <h1>Performance Limitations</h1>

<p>
    The Crusoe processor has a lot of limitations which can be seen from the
    references. Some of the key limitations are outlined as follows:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        The Crusoe processor was slow, especially with floating point
        instructions. It could only process one floating point instruction at a time. It
        has about 6 times slower execution time for floating point
        instructions than C3 Nehemiah on the floating-point multimedia
        benchmark.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        Though VLIW molecules can contain up to 4 atoms, it is not always
        possible to find 4 atoms to make a molecule. At some instances, only
        one atom executes per cycle, affecting the IPC.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        There is a tradeoff between power and clock frequency. Since the
        Crusoe processor was designed with low power in mind, it can only
        reach a maximum of 1 GHz clock frequency.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        The L2 cache, despite being large, is not large enough to store the
        Code Morphing Software and the translated code. This leads to cache
        thrashing.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
	The processor has about 16 MB of space reserved for the translation
    	cache. However, when a large program (with sizes typical for
    	real-world applications) is encountered, there is a huge translation
    	penalty because the translation cache cannot hold all of the translated
    	code.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        As outlined in the Vans Hardware article in the references, the
        processor also performs poorly on most of the benchmarks.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</Layout>
